!script {{
  --/| ==========================================================================
  --/| Tutorial 02: D&D 2024 Beacon Structured Reads
  --/|
  --/| PURPOSE
  --/| Direct aliases such as hp are convenient, but Beacon also stores a great
  --/| deal of information inside larger groups of sheet data. A structured read
  --/| lets ScriptCards follow an exact path to one value inside that data.
  --/|
  --/| HOW TO USE IT
  --/| 1. Select a token that represents a D&D 2024 Beacon NPC.
  --/| 2. Run this ScriptCard.
  --/| 3. Compare the direct and structured HP lines. They should show the same
  --/|    current HP even though they reach it in different ways.
  --/|
  --/| WHAT THIS TEACHES
  --/| [*S:hp] is a direct alias.
  --/| [*S:sheet->hitpoints->currentHP] is a structured read.
  --/|
  --/| The word sheet starts at the character's structured Beacon sheet data.
  --/| Each -> means "go one step deeper" until the final value is reached.
  --/|
  --/| sheet and store are two public names for the same structured current root.
  --/| In other words, these reach the same place:
  --/|
  --/| sheet->settings->rolls->mode
  --/| store->settings->rolls->mode
  --/|
  --/| IMPORTANT: rolls->mode is the GLOBAL d20 roll mode for the character.
  --/| It is not the per-skill/per-save Advantage or Disadvantage imposed by
  --/| armor, Conditions, Effects, or other rules. Targeted overrides are stored
  --/| in Roll Bonus records and are covered in Tutorial 17.
  --/|
  --/| THE CURRENT TREE AND THE MAX TREE
  --/| Structured Beacon data has a current tree and can also have a parallel max
  --/| tree. Appending ^ to a COMPLETE lookup asks for that same path in the max
  --/| tree.
  --/|
  --/| The ^ does not mean "calculate the maximum version of this value."
  --/| It literally means "look at this path in the max tree."
  --/|
  --/| Many D&D 2024 Beacon values have nothing stored in the max tree. That is
  --/| normal. Final values such as maximum HP can instead have their own direct
  --/| alias or be calculated from canonical records.
  --/|
  --/| Structured reads are useful when there is no simple direct alias, or when
  --/| you deliberately need one exact stored setting or value.
  --/|
  --/| This tutorial only reads information. It does not change the character.
  --/| ==========================================================================

  --#beaconsheet|1
  --#sourcetoken|@{selected|token_id}
  --#title|[*S:character_name] - Structured Reads Tutorial

  --+|[c][b]Direct Alias vs Structured Path[/b][/c]

  --+Direct HP|[*S:hp]
  --+Structured HP|[*S:sheet->hitpoints->currentHP]

  --+|[c][b]Hit Point Data[/b][/c]

  --+Current HP|[*S:sheet->hitpoints->currentHP]
  --+Temporary HP|[*S:sheet->hitpoints->tempHP]

  --+|[c][b]Character Information[/b][/c]

  --+Creature Type|[*S:sheet->character->creatureType]
  --+Alignment|[*S:sheet->about->characteristics->alignment]
  --+Size|[*S:sheet->about->characteristics->size]

  --+|[c][b]NPC Information[/b][/c]

  --+Challenge Rating|[*S:sheet->npc->challengeRating]
  --+HP Formula|[*S:sheet->npc->rollHP]
  --+Gear|[*S:sheet->npc->gear]
  --+Habitat|[*S:sheet->npc->habitat]
  --+Treasure|[*S:sheet->npc->treasure]

  --+|[c][b]sheet and store Reach the Same Root[/b][/c]

  --+Using sheet|[*S:sheet->settings->rolls->mode]
  --+Using store|[*S:store->settings->rolls->mode]

  --+|[c][b]Current Tree vs Max Tree[/b][/c]

  --/| The first line reads the normal current tree.
  --+Global d20 Roll Mode|[*S:sheet->settings->rolls->mode]

  --/| The second asks for the same path in the parallel max tree.
  --/| Most settings do not have a max-tree value, so the default text is normal.
  --+Same Path in Max Tree|[*S:sheet->settings->rolls->mode^:::No max-tree value]

  --+|[c][b]Other Sheet Settings[/b][/c]

  --+Roll Privacy|[*S:sheet->settings->rolls->privacy]
  --+Automatic Damage|[*S:sheet->settings->rollDamageAutomatic]
  --+Condition Token Sync|[*S:sheet->settings->useConditionTokenSync]
}}
